WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.880
On its own, gamma-P-G-A is a floppy tangle of chains that

2
00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:09.200
all carry a negative charge, so they repel and slide past

3
00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:12.400
each other. How do you turn that into a solid?

4
00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:18.360
Calcium is the answer. Each calcium ion carries two positive charges,

5
00:00:18.770 --> 00:00:21.620
so it can grab a negative site on one chain and

6
00:00:21.670 --> 00:00:25.220
a negative site on the next, at the same time.

7
00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:28.580
Each ion clamps two chains together into a bridge.

8
00:00:29.330 --> 00:00:35.900
How many bridges form? A binding curve tells us: as calcium rises, more

9
00:00:35.950 --> 00:00:40.660
sites get clamped, until the chains are saturated and it levels off.

10
00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:46.540
And bridge count is what sets stiffness. More clamps means a

11
00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:51.320
denser network, and a denser network is a stiffer, stronger gel.

12
00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:56.960
So the whole story is: count the calcium clamps, and that

13
00:00:57.010 --> 00:01:00.500
number becomes the stiffness the crust brings to the sand.
